<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu Abdallah al-Shi'i</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/911">911 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu Abdallah al-Shi'i was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismaili</a> missionary who converted and organised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutama-berbers">Kutama Berbers</a> of what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> in the late ninth century. Leading them in war, he overthrew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghlabid-dynasty">Aghlabid dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>. His campaign prepared the way for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdallah-al-mahdi-billah">Abdallah al-Mahdi Billah</a> to be proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, making him a key figure in the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abu Abdallah was born al-Husayn ibn Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Zakariyya in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. He may first have been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> or a Twelver Shi'a before he and his brother Abu'l-Abbas Muhammad were won over to Isma'ili Shi'ism in 891, by the local missionary Abu Ali or, in other accounts, by Hamdan Qarmat. Both joined the Isma'ili missionary network, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dawa">da'wa</a>. Abu Abdallah reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> in April 892 and studied with its chief missionary, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-hawshab">Ibn Hawshab</a>. Sent on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutama-berbers">Kutama Berbers</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">Hajj</a> and persuaded them to invite him home, though modern scholars doubt that the meeting was by chance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesser-kabylia">Lesser Kabylia</a> in June 893, worked first from Ikjan and then from Tazrut, and modelled his community on the example of Muhammad. Setting aside clan loyalties, he divided his armed followers into seven parts under appointed commanders and set aside a fifth of all booty for the awaited mahdi, a treasure he never touched. Late in 902 he took the fortified town of Mila, and in 904 Setif. His men destroyed an Aghlabid army at Kayuna, and Tubna capitulated in October 906 after a siege of almost a year.</p><p>After the sack of Billizma in the spring of 907 and the opening of Baghaya and Tijis, only al-Urbus stood before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a>. On 18 March 909 the last Aghlabid army was overwhelmed there and the inhabitants massacred; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziyadat-allah-iii">Ziyadat Allah III</a> fled, and on 25 March Abu Abdallah entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raqqada">Raqqada</a>. He issued a pardon to the citizens and changed the coinage, the call to prayer and the sermon. On 6 June he set out to free his master from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sijilmasa">Sijilmasa</a>, overthrowing the Rustamid imamate at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiaret">Tahert</a> on the way, and al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a> was enthroned on 27 August 909.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>, a former merchant used to wine and rich clothing, disappointed followers raised on Abu Abdallah's austere teaching, and Abu Abdallah himself accused him of corrupting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutama-berbers">Kutama</a> with power and luxury. With his brother and other Kutama chiefs he publicly charged al-Mahdi with being an impostor and demanded a miracle, and the conspirators then planned to kill him. Warned by the commander Ghazwiya, al-Mahdi moved first: on 18 February 911 Abu Abdallah and his brother were killed by loyal soldiers in the palace. He was given a formal funeral, and pro-Fatimid sources present his end as a loyal servant's fall through ambition.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abu Abdallah al-Shi'i” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 893 the Isma'ili Fatimid movement began among the Kutama Berbers, led by <b>Abu Abdallah al-Shi'i</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The da'i <b>Abu Abdallah al-Shi'i</b> won the Kutama Berbers of Ifriqiya to the cause of a hidden imam whose identity was kept secret for security.</blockquote><a href="/empire/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Between about 905 and 909 the future Fatimid caliph Ubayd Allah was held captive there until rescued by <b>Abu Abdallah al-Shi'i</b>, who executed the emir al-Yasa.</blockquote><a href="/state/midrarid-emirate">Midrarid emirate</a></li><li><blockquote>In 909 <b>Abu Abdallah al-Shi'i</b>, leading the Kutama armies of the Fatimid movement, captured Tahert.</blockquote><a href="/state/rustamid-imamate">Rustamid imamate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.03,44.4&amp;year=901" aria-label="Kufa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5693 998 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Abu Abdallah al-Shi'i"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6143 1268h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6143 1268h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=32.03,44.4&amp;year=901">What was here in 901 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
